    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          The question is not which sealer is better. Acrylic is better at almost everything. The question is whether your driveway is worth three to five times the money, and for a plain black residential drive the honest answer is usually no.
        </p>

        <h2 id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. What Each One Actually Is
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Asphalt Emulsion</h3>
        <p>
          An asphalt emulsion sealer is exactly what the name says: asphalt cement, the same petroleum binder that holds the stones in your driveway together, ground into microscopic droplets and suspended in water with the help of an emulsifying agent. To that base the manufacturer adds clay, usually a bentonite or attapulgite, which thickens the mix, keeps the droplets apart in the pail and gives the dried film body. Then come mineral fillers, some pigment to deepen the black, and in the better grades a few per cent of latex polymer. When you spread it, the water evaporates, the asphalt droplets coalesce back into a continuous film, and the clay and fillers are locked inside it. What you are left with is a thin layer of new, unoxidised asphalt on top of the old, oxidised asphalt. It is protecting the pavement with more of the same material, refreshed.
        </p>

        <p>
          That is both its strength and its limit. Because the film is asphalt, it bonds beautifully to asphalt, flexes with it and looks exactly like it. But it also ages like it. Ultraviolet light oxidises the film, gasoline dissolves it, and after a couple of seasons it has gone grey and thin in the tyre tracks, which is why the standard advice is to reseal every two to three years. The whole retail black sealer shelf at Home Depot and Lowe's, from the eighty-dollar economy pails to the premium Latex-ite and Black Jack grades, is asphalt emulsion of varying quality. Our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt sealer reviews</a> rank those grades.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Acrylic Sealer</h3>
        <p>
          An acrylic driveway sealer contains no asphalt at all. It is a water-based dispersion of acrylic polymer, the same family of resins that goes into exterior house paint, high-quality concrete sealers and the coloured coatings on tennis courts. When the water leaves, the polymer particles fuse into a tough, flexible, transparent plastic film. Colour comes from pigment dispersed in that film, so it can be any colour the manufacturer chooses to make. Black acrylic sealers exist and look very convincing on asphalt, but the chemistry does not care what colour it is.
        </p>

        <p>
          The acrylic film is fundamentally different from an emulsion film. It is harder, it does not oxidise in sunlight the way asphalt does, it is not dissolved by petroleum, and it holds its colour for years. It also does not become one with the asphalt beneath it; it sits on top as a distinct coating that is bonded mechanically and chemically to the surface. That means it depends much more heavily on surface preparation and on being applied in the right film thickness, and it means the failure mode is different. Emulsion wears away gradually. Acrylic, if it fails, peels.
        </p>

        <h2 id="coal-tar" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Where Coal Tar Fits In
        </h2>

        <p>
          You cannot talk about asphalt sealers for long without coal tar coming up, because for fifty years refined coal tar emulsion was the professional standard and asphalt emulsion was the cheap alternative. Coal tar uses refined coal tar pitch as the binder instead of petroleum asphalt. The pitch is chemically unlike petroleum, which is why gasoline and oil do not dissolve it, and it is harder and more UV-stable than asphalt. On performance alone it beats asphalt emulsion comfortably, and it is why older parking lots that were sealed with tar still look black long after the emulsion-sealed drive next door has gone grey.
        </p>

        <p>
          The problem is that refined coal tar is roughly half polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons by weight, and the dust that wears off a sealed surface carries those PAHs into house dust and stormwater. Enough jurisdictions have banned or restricted it, including Washington State, Minnesota, the District of Columbia and a long list of counties and cities, that the big retailers have largely stopped carrying it and the manufacturers have reformulated their premium lines as asphalt emulsion. We go through the whole debate in <a href="/coal-tar-vs-asphalt-emulsion-sealer/">coal tar versus asphalt emulsion sealer</a>. For this comparison, the relevant point is that coal tar sits above both acrylic and asphalt emulsion on fuel resistance, roughly between them on UV resistance, and is increasingly not an option at all. If you have been told to look at acrylic because coal tar is no longer sold in your area, that is a sensible reason to be reading this.
        </p>

        <h2 id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Durability, UV and Fuel Resistance
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Long Each Lasts</h3>
        <p>
          A good asphalt emulsion, applied in two coats over a clean, sound surface, lasts one to three years before the tyre tracks go grey and it wants another coat. The economy grades sit at the bottom of that range, the polymer-modified and sand-filled premium grades at the top. A properly applied acrylic system lasts three to five years on a residential drive and we have seen well-maintained coloured acrylic surfaces go considerably longer than that with a maintenance coat every few years. Those figures are for coatings that went on correctly. A sealer of either type applied over dirt, over damp asphalt, or over a surface with the previous coating flaking off will fail in a season, and the acrylic will fail more dramatically because it peels rather than wears. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-driveway-sealer-last/">how long driveway sealer lasts</a> covers the variables in detail.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ultraviolet Resistance</h3>
        <p>
          This is where acrylic pulls furthest ahead. Ultraviolet light breaks down asphalt by oxidising its lighter fractions, and that is why untreated asphalt goes from black to grey in two or three summers. An emulsion sealer is a sacrificial layer of fresh asphalt that takes that UV hit so the pavement does not, and it greys out in exactly the same way, just a little more slowly because of its pigment and fillers. Acrylic polymers are chosen for exterior coatings precisely because they are UV-stable; house paint would not last a decade otherwise. An acrylic sealer does not oxidise in sunlight to any meaningful degree, and because it is opaque it blocks UV from reaching the asphalt underneath. In the high-UV climates of the Southwest and Florida, this alone can justify the price.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Oil and Gasoline Resistance</h3>
        <p>
          Neither product is good at this in absolute terms; coal tar was the benchmark and both of these fall short of it. Asphalt emulsion is the weaker of the two by a wide margin, because asphalt is itself a petroleum product and gasoline is a solvent for it. A fuel spill on an emulsion-sealed drive softens the film and, if left, dissolves through it into the asphalt below. Acrylic is genuinely better. The cured polymer film is not attacked by gasoline the way asphalt is, and a spill wiped up within a reasonable time leaves little trace. Prolonged contact will still soften and stain it, and a chronic drip from a leaking sump will get through eventually. If fuel resistance is your main concern, acrylic is the better of the two available options, and keeping a drip tray under the leaky car is better than either.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Flexibility and Cracking</h3>
        <p>
          Asphalt moves. It expands in summer heat, contracts in winter cold and flexes slightly under load, and a sealer has to move with it or crack. Emulsion has the advantage here because it is asphalt; it moves in step with the pavement. Acrylic is flexible enough for the job when formulated properly, but a cheap or heavily applied acrylic can become brittle in cold and show hairline crazing over the winter. This is why the good acrylic products are applied thin and why the manufacturers are so insistent about film thickness.
        </p>

        <h2 id="colour" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Colour and Appearance
        </h2>

        <p>
          Asphalt emulsion is black. Manufacturers add carbon black to make it a deeper, richer black, and the premium grades dry to a handsome dark charcoal, but that is the entire palette. If you want a black driveway that looks like new asphalt, emulsion gives you that look convincingly, and for the first year it is hard to tell a freshly emulsion-sealed drive from a freshly paved one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Acrylic is where colour lives. Because the pigment sits in a clear polymer film, the manufacturer can make it any colour, and they do: the greens and reds of tennis courts, the tans and terracottas used on decorative drives and resort paths, the greys that make asphalt read as concrete from the street, and the bright colours on playground surfaces. This is not a novelty. In neighbourhoods with design covenants, on properties where a black driveway fights with the house, and anywhere the owner simply wants the drive to look like something other than a parking lot, acrylic is the only asphalt coating that can do it. The colour also holds. A coloured acrylic drive fades slowly and evenly over years rather than going patchy grey in eighteen months.
        </p>

        <p>
          Two cautions. Lighter acrylic colours show tyre marks, oil drips and leaf stains far more than black does, and they need more frequent cleaning to look their best. And a coloured acrylic coating over an asphalt drive is a commitment; once the driveway is tan, going back to black means either a black acrylic over it or waiting years for the acrylic to wear through, because emulsion does not bond well to it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Cost and Coverage
        </h2>

        <p>
          Asphalt emulsion is cheap. A five-gallon pail of economy grade runs in the low twenties of dollars at the big boxes, the premium polymer-modified and sand-filled grades run from roughly thirty-five to sixty, and a pail covers somewhere between two hundred and fifty and four hundred square feet per coat depending on how porous the surface is. Call it ten to twenty cents per square foot per coat in material, and twenty to forty cents for the two coats you should be applying. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/">what it costs to seal a driveway</a> puts a full DIY job on a typical two-car drive at well under two hundred dollars in material.
        </p>

        <p>
          Acrylic is a different price bracket. The quality asphalt-specific acrylics, the coloured court surfacing systems and the black acrylic sealers sold through paving suppliers typically run from around forty to ninety dollars a gallon, and although coverage per gallon is higher because the film is thinner, usually in the range of a hundred to two hundred square feet per gallon per coat, you still end up at something like fifty cents to well over a dollar per square foot for two coats. Multiply that across a six hundred square foot drive and the material bill is three to seven hundred dollars against a hundred and fifty for emulsion. A professional acrylic colour system, with the primer and filler coats the manufacturer specifies, is more again.
        </p>

        <p>
          The way to reconcile those numbers is by years of service. Emulsion at forty cents per square foot every two years is twenty cents per square foot per year. Acrylic at ninety cents every four years is about twenty-two cents. On pure cost per year they are closer than the pail prices suggest, and acrylic pulls ahead if it genuinely goes five years. But that arithmetic only works if the acrylic is applied well enough to reach its lifespan, and the emulsion route spreads the cost out and lets you fix mistakes cheaply.
        </p>

        <h2 id="application" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Application and Compatibility
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Putting Down Emulsion</h3>
        <p>
          Emulsion is forgiving. You pour a ribbon across the drive and pull it out with a squeegee or a squeegee-brush, and the mix has enough body that minor unevenness levels itself. It is thick, it goes on at a relatively heavy film, it can carry <a href="/sand-additive-for-driveway-sealer/">sand for texture and wear</a>, and a second coat over the first is straightforward once the first has dried. Our <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">step-by-step guide</a> is written around it. The main things that go wrong are applying it too thick, which leaves it soft for days, and applying it over dirt or damp, which makes it peel.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Putting Down Acrylic</h3>
        <p>
          Acrylic is applied like a coating, not a sealer. Thin, even coats, usually two and sometimes three, with a roller, a soft rubber squeegee or an airless sprayer. The film build per coat is a fraction of what an emulsion lays down, and the product is much less tolerant of puddling; a heavy patch of acrylic will skin over, trap water beneath, and crack or turn milky. It dries fast, often within an hour in warm weather, so you work in smaller sections and keep a wet edge to avoid lap marks. Acrylic does not normally carry sand mixed into the bucket; where texture is wanted, a fine silica is broadcast onto the wet coat or the manufacturer supplies a textured version. The surface has to be clean to a higher standard than emulsion demands, because the coating relies entirely on adhesion, and any oil spot or loose dust becomes a peel point. Some systems specify a primer over bare or heavily oxidised asphalt.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Going Over Old Coatings</h3>
        <p>
          Acrylic over cured, well-bonded emulsion generally works. The polymer bonds to the weathered emulsion film acceptably, and this is the route most people take when upgrading a driveway that has been emulsion-sealed for years. The old coating must be intact; anything flaking has to come off first, and <a href="/how-to-remove-old-driveway-sealer/">removing old sealer</a> is not a small job. Emulsion over acrylic is the problem direction. Asphalt emulsion does not wet out on a slick polymer surface and the bond is poor, so the emulsion tends to lift in sheets within a year. If the drive has ever had an acrylic coating, stay in the acrylic family. Neither product should go over an old coal tar coating without checking the manufacturer's compatibility guidance, because tar and asphalt are not fully compatible and acrylic adhesion to tar varies.
        </p>

        <h2 id="polymer-modified" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          7. What Polymer-Modified Really Means
        </h2>

        <p>
          Walk the sealer aisle and half the pails say polymer-modified, latex-fortified, rubberised or acrylic-enhanced. It is worth being clear about what that is and is not. These are asphalt emulsions with a small quantity of latex polymer blended in at the factory, typically a few per cent by volume of an acrylic or a styrene-butadiene rubber latex. The polymer does several useful things: it makes the dried film more flexible so it moves with the asphalt through freeze-thaw cycles without cracking; it speeds the initial set, so the coat is rain-safe sooner; it improves adhesion to the pavement and to the previous coat; and it toughens the film so sand stays bound and the tyre tracks wear through more slowly.
        </p>

        <p>
          What it does not do is turn an emulsion into an acrylic sealer. The binder is still asphalt. It still oxidises in the sun, gasoline still dissolves it, and it still lasts two to three years rather than four or five. A latex-fortified emulsion is a better emulsion, not a cheap acrylic, and the marketing sometimes blurs that line. If the pail is black, sells for under seventy dollars for five gallons, and is spread with a squeegee at three hundred square feet a pail, it is an emulsion however many polymer words are on the label.
        </p>

        <p>
          Contractors achieve the same effect by adding latex fortifier to a plain emulsion at the truck, and the <a href="/commercial-asphalt-sealer/">commercial-grade products</a> are typically sold that way. For a homeowner, the premium retail grades that already include the polymer are the sensible buy, and they are what we recommend for the standard black driveway in almost every case.
        </p>

        <h2 id="best-use-cases" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          8. Which One for Your Driveway
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Choose Asphalt Emulsion When</h3>
        <p>
          Your driveway is standard black asphalt in reasonable condition and you want to keep it black, protected and looking maintained. You are comfortable resealing every two to three years and would rather spend a hundred and fifty dollars every couple of years than six hundred every four or five. You are doing the work yourself with a squeegee and want a forgiving material. The drive has been emulsion-sealed before. You live in a climate with hard winters where flexibility matters more than UV resistance. This describes the large majority of driveways in the country, and for them a polymer-modified, sand-filled emulsion from our <a href="/best-driveway-sealer/">best driveway sealer</a> list is the right call.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Choose Acrylic When</h3>
        <p>
          You want a colour other than black. The driveway is a decorative or high-end surface, or it abuts stamped concrete, pavers or a coloured path and you want it to harmonise rather than contrast. You are in a high-UV climate and tired of resealing every other summer. You have a chronic fuel or oil issue and want the better of the two available resistances. You are willing to prepare the surface meticulously and apply thin coats carefully, or you are paying a contractor who does this regularly. You want to seal a fresh overlay to a high standard and leave it alone for several years. Acrylic is also the right family if the drive has ever been coated with acrylic before, regardless of anything else.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Choose Neither When</h3>
        <p>
          The driveway is alligatored, ravelling badly, has depressions holding water or has structural cracks. No sealer of any chemistry fixes those. The <a href="/driveway-sealer-vs-resurfacer/">sealer versus resurfacer</a> question comes first, and the sealer is the last step after the repair, not a substitute for it. Spending the acrylic premium on a failing surface is the most expensive way there is to buy one more year.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Treating latex-fortified as acrylic</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A polymer-modified emulsion is an emulsion with a little latex in it. It is a better black sealer, not a long-life acrylic coating, and it will not last five years.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Applying acrylic like emulsion</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Thick ribbons pulled out with a squeegee are the emulsion method. Acrylic wants thin, even coats with a roller or sprayer. Puddled acrylic hazes, traps water and cracks.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Putting emulsion over an acrylic-coated drive</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Asphalt emulsion does not bond to a polymer film. It lifts in sheets within a year. Once a drive is acrylic, it stays acrylic.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Buying acrylic to fix a failing surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Neither product repairs anything. Cracks, ravelling and depressions need filler or a resurfacer first. An expensive coating on a bad base is the most expensive way to get one more season.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Skimping on prep because acrylic is tougher</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The opposite is true. Acrylic relies entirely on adhesion and peels from any oil spot or dust film. It needs a cleaner surface than emulsion, not a dirtier one.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is acrylic driveway sealer better than asphalt emulsion?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Acrylic lasts longer, holds colour better and resists UV and fuel better, but it costs three to five times as much per gallon and is less forgiving to apply. For a standard black asphalt driveway that needs routine protection, a good polymer-modified asphalt emulsion is the better value. Acrylic earns its price on decorative, coloured or high-end surfaces.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does acrylic driveway sealer last compared to asphalt emulsion?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A properly applied two-coat acrylic system typically lasts three to five years and often longer before it needs a recoat. Asphalt emulsion lasts one to three years depending on the grade, the sand load and the traffic. Neither figure holds if the surface was dirty or damp when the sealer went on.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put acrylic sealer over an old asphalt emulsion sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Usually yes, once the old emulsion is fully cured, clean and well bonded. Acrylics adhere well to weathered emulsion. The reverse, emulsion over acrylic, is less reliable because emulsion does not bond well to a slick polymer film and can peel in sheets. Check the acrylic manufacturer's instructions for any primer requirement.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a polymer-modified or latex-fortified asphalt emulsion?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">It is a standard asphalt emulsion with a few per cent of acrylic or styrene-butadiene latex blended in. The polymer improves flexibility, adhesion, cure speed and wear resistance. It is not an acrylic sealer; it is an emulsion with a small dose of the same chemistry, and its performance sits between the two.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does acrylic sealer come in colours?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes. Acrylic is the only common asphalt sealer that is offered in a full colour range, including greys, tans, reds, greens and blues, because the pigment sits in a clear polymer film rather than a black asphalt binder. That is why tennis courts, playgrounds and decorative drives are coated with acrylic.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Which sealer resists oil and gasoline better?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Acrylic resists petroleum spills better than asphalt emulsion, because asphalt is itself a petroleum product and dissolves in gasoline. Neither matches refined coal tar, which is the benchmark for fuel resistance. Wipe any spill promptly regardless of the sealer.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you apply acrylic driveway sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">In thin coats, usually two, with a roller, a soft squeegee or an airless sprayer. Acrylic is applied at a much lower film build than emulsion and it dries fast, so you work in smaller sections and keep a wet edge. Heavy coats or puddles will crack and haze.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is asphalt emulsion the same as coal tar sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">No. Coal tar emulsion uses refined coal tar pitch as the binder, which is harder and more fuel resistant but is banned in many jurisdictions over PAH concerns. Asphalt emulsion uses petroleum asphalt as the binder and is the standard black sealer sold at retail today.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

